    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm doing some prep for my clinical rotations and wanted to go over some diagnostic patterns. When looking at dermatological conditions like erythema or certain types of skin cancer, how does the typical presentation change based on the patient's skin phototype? Like, what should I be looking for in darker skin tones compared to the standard presentations in textbooks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, your observation is spot on; phototype significantly alters the clinical presentation and early detection of dermatological conditions in darker skin tones. For instance, mucosal melanoma often appears as axillary or ocular lesions rather than on sun-exposed areas, leading to severe underestimation of its spread, while invasive basal cell carcinoma frequently presents as pearly tubercles at the hair line or scalp rather than the classic face or ears. Additionally, signs of cutaneous photodamage or even tumors like squamous cell carcinoma may be harder to visualize due to the lack of distinct erythema, meaning clinicians must rely on subtle textural changes or consult specialized diagnostic tools. Ultimately, preparing for these variations is critical because typical textbook images based on lighter skin can lead to delayed diagnoses if not actively considered during your rotations.",
